  "query_preview": "..(Saturday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 255 AM EDT FRI AUG 21 2026\n\nThe long term period will open with deep upper level troughing over\nthe eastern CONUS and a high amplitude upper level ridge centered\nnear the Four Corners region. At the surface, convection will be \nexiting far eastern Kentucky as the previous system departs. \nMeanwhile, a surface low will be located over the Great Lakes, \nwith an additional cold front draped to the southwest. As this low\nlifts northeastward, its associated front will progress eastward \ninto the forecast area on Sunday. Unlike the previous system, the \nfrontal passage is expected to remain dry, owing to limited \nmoisture ahead of the front. The main impact will be cooler and \ndrier weather behind this boundary; dewpoints will fall into the \n50s, and high temperatures will struggle to reach beyond the upper\n70s to low 80s on Sunday afternoon. Overnight, clear skies and a \nweak pressure gradient will allow a modest ridge/valley \ntemperature split to develop. MOS guidance seems slightly \naggressive with this cooling, but given ideal radiational cooling \nconditions, it seems plausible that low temperatures could fall \ninto the mid 50s for valley locations, with typical fog formation.\n\nForecast uncertainty increases on Monday and Tuesday as eastern \nKentucky finds itself on the western fringes of the progressing \ntrough. Within increasingly northwesterly flow aloft, some model \nguidance suggests shortwave energy could spark additional rain chances\nas early as Monday afternoon, although ensembles still favor an \nanomalously dry environment through Tuesday overall. Given limited\nagreement in the evolution of these smaller scale impulses, PoPs \nremain low, with the exception of the southern portion of the \nforecast area where a slight chance of showers and storms was \nintroduced. Although this highlights a marginally higher consensus\nregarding modest rain chances, forecast confidence is still low, \nand any coverage details will bear refining in the coming days. \nBarring convective interference, highs should still remain in the \nupper 70s to low 80s on Monday afternoon, with low temperatures in\nthe mid to upper 50s across the forecast area overnight. On \nTuesday, the eastward expansion of the upper level ridge will \nallow temperatures to climb a few degrees, with highs ranging from\nthe low to mid 80s.\n\nMore robust rain chances will return by midweek as a closed upper\nlevel low progresses to the southeast from Manitoba into the Upper\nMidwest. In turn, deepening upper level troughing over the Midwest\nwill lead to broad height falls across the Ohio Valley, with\nsouthwesterly to westerly surface winds allowing dewpoints to rise\nback into the upper 60s and low 70s. This, in combination with the\npotential for additional shortwave activity, will maintain PoPs in\nthe neighborhood of 30-40% on both Wednesday and Thursday.",
